From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Crunch41 <70764418+Crunch41@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 21:34:38 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] Log room activity and transport diagnostics
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

In a container log, what matters is who joined, left, chatted and
played, and when. Room activity now starts with a tag (JOIN, LEAVE,
KICK, BAN, CHAT, GAME, STAT, DIAG), which the log shows as a fixed
label (the STAT line is one line in the log, wrapped here):

  [10:23:45] JOIN   PlayerName joined  1/16 · 172ms · 1.2.3.4
  [10:23:46] GAME   PlayerName is playing Mario Kart 8 Deluxe (3.0.3)
  [10:24:10] CHAT   PlayerName: gg
  [10:27:57] STAT   [1.2.3.4] PlayerName session join_rtt 172ms
      last_rtt 180ms peak_rtt 210ms game_pkts 41800 up_holds 12
      (2950ms, max 260ms) gaps 14 (max 410ms, 2 unheld) burst_max 46
      duration 4m12s
  [10:27:57] LEAVE  PlayerName left after 4m12s · played Mario Kart 8
      Deluxe · ping 180ms · some lag spikes  0/16
  [10:31:02] JOIN   Visitor joined  1/16 · 220ms · 5.6.7.8
  [10:31:02] LEAVE  Visitor left after 0s, didn't play  0/16

- Only Info messages of the Network class whose first word is one of
  the tags get a label; everything else is logged as before.
- Join, leave, kick and ban lines show the player count and room size;
  join, kick and ban lines the address, and the join line the RTT.
- The LEAVE line sums up the session: how long, the last game played,
  RTT, and how the member's connection felt (below). A member that sent
  fewer than 100 game packets "didn't play": lobby probes and games'
  network scans.
- A game the client can't name ("Unknown program") is logged as an
  unknown game.
- STAT, before a member that sent game packets leaves, has the raw
  figures: RTT at join, last and peak RTT, what the room saw of the
  member's game packets (below) and session length. ENet resets a
  peer's statistics before reporting its disconnect, so RTT is kept
  from the join and from DIAG samples; with DIAG off, last and peak
  equal the join value.

What the room can see of a member's game packets on their way to it:

- Eden clients send everything reliably on one ENet channel, so when a
  datagram from a member is lost, ENet holds everything the member sends
  after it until the client resends it, one retransmit timeout later
  (about 50 ms on a short route, 250 ms across the Pacific). Every
  other player sees that member freeze, then jump. The room watches the
  channel's queue of held commands on every pass of its loop: a hold of
  10 ms or more is one up_hold (shorter ones are fragmented packets
  still arriving). This is the member's uplink loss as it affects play.
- A gap of 100 ms to 5 s between a member's game packets with no hold
  in it ("unheld") means the member sent nothing: their PC or emulation
  stalled (shader compiles, frame drops). Longer gaps are idle time.
- burst_max is the most game packets in a row less than 2 ms apart; a
  hold or a stall ends in a burst.

The LEAVE line's rating comes from the holds alone, for a session of a
minute or more: "laggy connection" when the member was held 1% of the
time or 3 times a minute, "some lag spikes" when held once a minute or
once for half a second, "connection good" otherwise. On the 146
sessions long enough to rate in a public room's logs, this gave 98
good, 37 with spikes and 11 laggy.

What it cannot see: loss on its own unreliable packets to members
(nothing acknowledges them), and anything in the game. ENet's packet
loss figure is the share of the room's sends it had to resend; in the
unreliable modes only pings and control messages are ever resent, so
it read about 0% on a path losing 1%. It is reported only as "resend"
in reliable mode.

EDEN_ROOM_SESSIONS_FILE (default empty = off) names a file that gets
one JSON line per finished session, for stats tools: name, address,
join and leave time (UTC), length, how it ended (left, kicked, banned,
room stopped), whether the member played, time in each game, RTT,
rating and the hold and gap figures. Members still in the room when it
stops are recorded too.

EDEN_ROOM_DIAG_INTERVAL_SEC (default 0 = off, up to 3600) adds a DIAG
report every that many seconds: relay mode and settings once at start,
then member RTT, holds and gaps, relayed packets and bytes (in total and
since the last report), drops by reason (oversize, malformed, budget,
unknown IP) and packet sizes; a line per member while game packets
flow; and an advice line that says only what those numbers show: who
is held or stalling, and, in the unreliable modes, what the room cannot
see. When it finds nothing wrong, it says only that the room sees no
stalls, never that the connection is fine.

Tests: tests/test_room_protocol.py checks the JOIN, LEAVE, KICK, CHAT,
GAME and STAT lines and the DIAG lines in the room's output, the
sessions file's records for a leave, a kick and a member still there
when the room stops, that a clean path shows no holds, and, for a
member behind a link that delays its packets by 30 ms, loses one and
then pauses, that DIAG and STAT show the loss as a hold and the pause
as an unheld gap and name the member.
